# Webhooks

> Success — sent to `callbacks.onSigned`

```http
POST /hooks/signature/signed HTTP/1.1
Host: app.example.com
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer 6f1c0b4e9a2d…

{
  "signatureRequestId": "3f1c7d2a-5a6e-4f5e-9d0b-0d7e9b1c2a11",
  "externalReference": "hr/contracts/4711",
  "clientId": "{{CLIENT_ID}}",
  "state": "Signed",
  "success": true,
  "signatureLevel": "QES",
  "provider": "FinaRiws",
  "documentName": "Employment contract - Ivana Horvat.pdf",
  "errorCode": null,
  "message": null,
  "signedAt": "2026-11-24T10:04:12Z",
  "finishedAt": "2026-11-24T10:04:12Z"
}
```

> Failure — sent to `callbacks.onError`

```json
{
  "signatureRequestId": "3f1c7d2a-5a6e-4f5e-9d0b-0d7e9b1c2a11",
  "externalReference": "hr/contracts/4711",
  "clientId": "{{CLIENT_ID}}",
  "state": "Rejected",
  "success": false,
  "signatureLevel": "QES",
  "provider": null,
  "documentName": "Employment contract - Ivana Horvat.pdf",
  "errorCode": "StandaloneIntegrationSignatureRejected",
  "message": "I do not agree with clause 4",
  "signedAt": null,
  "finishedAt": "2026-11-24T10:02:51Z"
}
```

When a request reaches its outcome, the Standalone Signer calls **one** of your URLs with the configured method, `Content-Type: application/json` and your `authorizationHeader` (if set).

| Field | Description |
|---|---|
| `signatureRequestId` | The request. |
| `externalReference` | Your reference from the create call. |
| `clientId` | The API client that created the request (useful when several clients share one webhook). |
| `state` | `Signed`, `Failed`, `Rejected` or `Expired`. |
| `success` | `true` only for `Signed`. |
| `signatureLevel`, `provider` | Level of the request and the provider used (if signing started). |
| `documentName` | Your document name. |
| `errorCode`, `message` | Error **name** and human-readable reason for unsuccessful outcomes. |
| `signedAt`, `finishedAt` | When the document was signed / when the request reached its outcome. |

**Delivery**

* Any `2xx` response counts as delivered. Each attempt times out after 15 seconds.
* Failed attempts are retried after 30 s, 60 s, 180 s and 300 s (5 attempts in total). After that the failure is recorded in the request's audit log and an operator can re-send the webhook.
* Delivery is **at-least-once**: make your handler idempotent, keyed on `signatureRequestId`.
* The webhook does not contain the PDF. Download it with [GET /integration/{id}/document](#download-the-document).
* The webhook is not signed. Authenticate it with the `authorizationHeader` you set when creating the request and treat it as a trigger: confirm with [GET /integration/{id}](#get-a-signature-request) if in doubt.

## Example webhook handler

```shell
# Simulate a webhook against your own endpoint
curl -X POST "https://app.example.com/hooks/signature/signed" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer 6f1c0b4e9a2d…" \
  -d '{"signatureRequestId":"3f1c7d2a-5a6e-4f5e-9d0b-0d7e9b1c2a11","state":"Signed","success":true}'
```

```csharp
app.MapPost("/hooks/signature/{outcome}", async (string outcome, SignatureCallback payload,
    HttpRequest request, DocumentStore store, StandaloneSignerClient signer) =>
{
    var document = await store.FindBySignatureRequestIdAsync(payload.SignatureRequestId);
    if (document is null) return Results.NotFound();

    // Authenticate with the per-request secret stored when the request was created.
    if (!CryptographicOperations.FixedTimeEquals(
            Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(request.Headers.Authorization.ToString()),
            Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes($"Bearer {document.WebhookSecret}")))
        return Results.Unauthorized();

    if (document.Status is DocumentStatus.Signed or DocumentStatus.Failed)
        return Results.Ok(); // idempotent: already processed

    if (payload.Success)
    {
        var pdf = await signer.DownloadDocumentAsync(payload.SignatureRequestId);
        await store.SaveSignedAsync(document, pdf);
        await signer.DeleteDocumentAsync(payload.SignatureRequestId);
    }
    else
    {
        await store.MarkFailedAsync(document, payload.State, payload.ErrorCode, payload.Message);
    }

    return Results.Ok();
});

public record SignatureCallback(Guid SignatureRequestId, string? ExternalReference, string State,
    bool Success, string? ErrorCode, string? Message);
```

```javascript
// Express
app.post('/hooks/signature/:outcome', express.json(), async (req, res) => {
  const payload = req.body;
  const doc = await store.findBySignatureRequestId(payload.signatureRequestId);
  if (!doc) return res.sendStatus(404);
  if (req.get('Authorization') !== `Bearer ${doc.webhookSecret}`) return res.sendStatus(401);
  if (doc.status === 'signed' || doc.status === 'failed') return res.sendStatus(200); // idempotent

  if (payload.success) {
    const pdf = await signer.downloadDocument(payload.signatureRequestId);
    await store.saveSigned(doc, pdf);
    await signer.deleteDocument(payload.signatureRequestId);
  } else {
    await store.markFailed(doc, payload.state, payload.errorCode, payload.message);
  }
  res.sendStatus(200);
});
```

Respond quickly with `2xx` and do slow work (downloads, notifications) after acknowledging if your platform allows it; the Standalone Signer waits at most 15 seconds per attempt.

# Signing UI (JavaScript SDK)

```html
<div id="signer" style="height: 80vh"></div>
<script src="https://{{SIGNER_HOST}}/signature/standalone/sdk/signator-signer-sdk.js"></script>
<script>
  const signer = SignatorSigner.create({
    container: '#signer',
    backendUrl: 'https://{{SIGNER_HOST}}',
    signatureToken: signatureTokenFromYourBackend,
    locale: 'hr',
    signatureText: { lineOne: 'Ivana Horvat', lineTwo: 'Employee' },
    onSuccess: (result) => showThankYou(result.signatureRequestId),
    onError: (result) => showError(result.state, result.errorCode, result.message),
    onStatus: (status) => console.debug('signer status', status)
  });
</script>
```

The SDK is a single script served by the Standalone Signer. `SignatorSigner.create` embeds an iframe (served by the Standalone Signer) in your container and passes the configuration to it with `postMessage`, so the signature token never appears in a URL. Inside the frame the signer sees the PDF (zoom, paging), positions the signature if you asked for that, chooses the provider if several are allowed and signs. When the request settles, the SDK calls `onSuccess` or `onError` once and removes the iframe.

The signing policy is not configured in the SDK: the SDK reads the level, the allowed providers and whether identification is allowed from the request your backend created.

## SDK options

| Option | Default | Description |
|---|---|---|
| `container` | required | Element or CSS selector. Give it an explicit height. |
| `backendUrl` | required | `https://{{SIGNER_HOST}}` |
| `signatureToken` | required | `signatureToken` from the create call. |
| `onSuccess(result)` | required | Called once when the document is signed. |
| `onError(result)` | required | Called once when signing failed, was rejected or expired, or the component could not start. |
| `onStatus(status)` | — | Progress events (`{ status, kind }`, plus `consent-redirect` and `identification-redirect`). |
| `locale` | browser language, else `en` | UI language: `en`, `hr`, `sl`, `de`, `fr`, `it`. |
| `language` | `EN` | Language of the provider's consent page, where the provider supports it. |
| `provider` | — | Preferred provider. Used without asking when the request allows it; otherwise ignored. |
| `providerLabels` | — | Your own labels for the provider chooser, e.g. `{ FinaRiws: 'FINA (mobile)' }`. |
| `signatureText` | — | `{ lineOne, lineTwo, lineThree, lineFour, useHandwrittenStyle }`: the signer positions a generated signature card. |
| `signatureImage` | — | Base64 or data-URL image (PNG/JPEG) the signer positions. |
| `signaturePosition` | position from the create call, else none | `{ page, x, y, width, height }` in PDF points (bottom-left origin). With a position the signature is shown there when the document opens; without one the signer places it (**Position image**). |
| `lockSignaturePosition` | `false` | Keeps the signature at `signaturePosition`: no drag or resize. This only locks the UI; to enforce a position, set `lockSignaturePosition` in the create call. |
| `allowReject` | `false` | Show a **Reject** button; the signer can decline with an optional reason. |
| `mode` | `iframe` | `popup` opens the signer in a separate window instead of the container. |
| `propagateErrors` | `true` | `false`: the SDK shows its own error dialog (with a support identifier) instead of calling `onError`. |
| `successfulSignatureRedirect`, `unsuccessfulSignatureRedirect` | bundled pages | Pages the provider returns the signer to. Keep the defaults unless you host your own (see below). |
| `pdfJsUrl`, `pdfWorkerUrl` | PDF.js 4.0.379 from cdnjs | Point to self-hosted copies for on-premise or strict-CSP deployments. |
| `pollIntervalMs`, `pollMaxMs` | `2000`, `30000` | Status polling back-off. |
| `frameUrl` | `{backendUrl}/signature/standalone/sdk/signer-frame.html` | Override only for custom hosting. |
| `debug` | `false` | Verbose logging; enables `onPosition` events with the placed coordinates. |

Methods: `signer.destroy()` removes the component (e.g. when the user navigates away).

<aside class="notice">
Signature placement: a signature with a known position (from the create call or <code>signaturePosition</code>) is shown on the document as soon as it opens, movable unless locked. A signature without a position is placed by the signer (<b>Position image</b> → drag/resize → <b>Sign</b>). Without any signature the signer only clicks <b>Sign</b>.
</aside>

## Result object

```json
{
  "signatureRequestId": "3f1c7d2a-5a6e-4f5e-9d0b-0d7e9b1c2a11",
  "state": "Signed",
  "success": true,
  "errorCode": null,
  "message": null,
  "source": "postMessage"
}
```

`onSuccess` and `onError` receive the same object. `state` is the request state when known (`Signed`, `Failed`, `Rejected`, `Expired`); `errorCode` is the error name, or `SDK_INIT_FAILED` when the component could not start.

<aside class="warning">
<code>onSuccess</code> is a UI signal for showing a confirmation. The authoritative outcome is the webhook to your backend (or <code>GET /integration/{id}</code>). Never mark a document as signed based only on a browser event.
</aside>

## What the signer experiences

* **Preset signature:** a signature with a position appears on the document right away — fixed when the request locks it, otherwise movable.
* **SES (seal):** **Sign** seals the document immediately; no provider page.
* **QES/AES with one allowed provider:** **Sign** starts the provider directly.
* **QES/AES with several providers:** **Sign** opens a chooser listing only the providers you allowed, in your order, with localized names (override with `providerLabels`).
* **Provider consent:** the frame navigates to the provider's page (FINA mobile approval, Certilia, Swisscom, A-Trust login…). When the signer finishes, the provider returns to the redirect page, which notifies the SDK. Status polling is a fallback if that message is lost.
* **Smart card / token (`LocalComponentV1`):** the consent page checks whether the ViziBit local component is running on the signer's computer and offers installers for Windows, macOS and Linux when it is missing or outdated.
* **Identification:** when the request allows it, the chooser also offers **"I don't have a digital identity"** (see [Identification](#identification-of-signers-without-a-digital-identity)).
* **Reject:** with `allowReject: true`, a **Reject** button asks for an optional reason; `onError` is then called with `state: "Rejected"`.
* **Reopening:** if the request already reached an outcome, the SDK reports it immediately. If signing already started (e.g. the page was reloaded), the signer can continue at the provider instead of starting again.

Some provider pages refuse to be displayed inside an iframe. If a provider page does not render in your container, use `mode: 'popup'`.

## Languages

The SDK UI is available in English (`en`), Croatian (`hr`), Slovenian (`sl`), German (`de`), French (`fr`) and Italian (`it`). Without `locale`, the browser language is used, falling back to English. The local component check page follows the browser language as well.

## Hosting, CSP and redirects

* Allow the Standalone Signer origin in your Content Security Policy for `script-src` and `frame-src`. The frame loads PDF.js from cdnjs unless you set `pdfJsUrl` and `pdfWorkerUrl`.
* Keep the default redirect pages. If you host your own, they must `postMessage({ type: 'SIGNATOR_SIGNING_SUCCESS' })` or `postMessage({ type: 'SIGNATOR_SIGNING_ERROR', errorCode })` to `window.parent` and `window.opener`; their origin is trusted automatically.
* The SDK needs no cookies. CORS is open on the Standalone Signer.
* Mobile apps can host the SDK in a WebView that allows pop-ups/new windows for provider pages.

# Signer endpoints

These endpoints use the **signature token** and are what the SDK calls. You only need them if you build your own signing UI or sign server-side (seals).

## Get the signing state

```shell
curl "https://{{SIGNER_HOST}}/v/1/signature/standalone/pdf/state" \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $SIGNATURE_TOKEN"
```

> Response `200 OK`

```json
{
  "signatureRequestId": "3f1c7d2a-5a6e-4f5e-9d0b-0d7e9b1c2a11",
  "state": "WaitingForUserApproval",
  "signatureLevel": "QES",
  "providers": ["FinaRiws", "CertiliaSdurddV1", "LocalComponentV1"],
  "provider": "CertiliaSdurddV1",
  "identificationAllowed": false,
  "documentName": "Employment contract - Ivana Horvat.pdf",
  "presetSignature": { "appearance": "CARD", "page": 3, "x": 355, "y": 40, "width": 200, "height": 70, "locked": true },
  "consentUrl": "https://provider.example/consent/…",
  "errorCode": null,
  "message": null,
  "expiresAt": "2026-12-31T23:00:00Z",
  "documentDeletedAt": null
}
```

`GET /v/1/signature/standalone/pdf/state` (also accepts `POST`)

The policy the signing UI may offer plus progress. `presetSignature` describes the signature from the create call (`null` if none). `consentUrl` appears a few seconds after a QES/AES signature was started. `externalReference` is deliberately not exposed to the signer.

## Get the preset signature image

`GET /v/1/signature/standalone/pdf/signature_image` (also accepts `POST`) — the preset signature as an image for display: your own image for `appearance` `0` (exactly what is stamped), otherwise a PNG of the generated card. `404` when the request has no preset signature.

## Download the document for preview

`GET /v/1/signature/standalone/pdf/download` (also accepts `POST`) — the current PDF.

## Start signing

```shell
curl -X POST "https://{{SIGNER_HOST}}/v/1/signature/standalone/pdf/init" \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $SIGNATURE_TOKEN" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{ "provider": "CertiliaSdurddV1", "language": "HR" }'
```

`POST /v/1/signature/standalone/pdf/init` → `204`

| Field | Description |
|---|---|
| `provider` | One of the request's `providers`. May be omitted when only one is allowed. Ignored for `SES`. |
| `signatureImage` | Optional placement and appearance replacing the one from the create call. |
| `signaturePosition` | Optional `{ page, x, y, width, height }` moving the signature (keeping its appearance). |
| `language` | Provider consent-page language (default `EN`). |
| `successfulSignatureRedirect`, `unsuccessfulSignatureRedirect` | Pages the provider returns to (the SDK sends its bundled pages). |

Outcomes: for `SES` the document is sealed during the call (state `Signed`, webhook queued). For `QES`/`AES` the state becomes `WaitingForUserApproval` and `consentUrl` follows. Errors: `400` (`186`) provider required, `403` (`205`) provider not allowed for the request or signature position locked, `409` already started or finished, `410` document deleted.

## Reject as the signer

`POST /v/1/signature/standalone/pdf/reject` with `{ "reason": "…" }` (optional) → `204`.

## Start identification

`POST /v/1/signature/standalone/pdf/identification` with `{ "returnUrl": "…", "language": "HR" }` (optional) → `200 { "redirectUrl": "…" }`. See [Identification](#identification-of-signers-without-a-digital-identity).

# Electronic seals

An electronic seal (`signatureLevel: "SES"`) is applied by the ViziBit Signing Service with a seal certificate, without any user interaction. Two kinds of seal are available:

| Seal | `sealId` | The seal certificate names | Typical use |
|---|---|---|---|
| **ViziBit electronic seal** | `Generic` | ViziBit | Protects the document against changes and shows that it was sealed through the ViziBit platform, e.g. on confirmations, receipts and protocols. Available to every API client without setup. |
| **Your organisation's seal** | assigned by ViziBit, e.g. `acme-hr-seal` | Your organisation | Documents that must carry your organisation's seal, e.g. an employer seal on confirmations and decisions, or on a contract after the employee signed. |

Which seal is applied:

* With `sealId`: that seal, if your client may use it (`Generic` or a seal assigned to your client); otherwise `403` (`errorCode` `205`).
* Without `sealId`: the seal assigned to your client if exactly one is assigned; `Generic` if none is assigned; `400` (`errorCode` `275`) if several are assigned.

The applied seal is returned as `sealId` in the create response and in the [request details](#get-a-signature-request), and is shown on the dashboard.

<aside class="notice">
When a document must carry your organisation's seal, always send its <code>sealId</code>. A request without <code>sealId</code> is sealed with the ViziBit seal whenever no seal is assigned to your client at that moment, for example after your seal was withdrawn.
</aside>

## How organisation seals are provisioned

1. **Certificate.** The organisation obtains an electronic seal certificate (a qualified seal certificate from a qualified trust service provider when the seal must have qualified status) as a PKCS#12 file. In the sandbox, ViziBit can assign a test seal.
2. **Installation.** ViziBit installs the certificate in the ViziBit Signing Service under a seal id unique to the organisation (e.g. `acme-hr-seal`). The certificate never leaves the Signing Service.
3. **Assignment.** The seal id is assigned to the organisation's API client (`sealIds`, see [Administration](#administration-api-clients)). A client can only use the seals assigned to it and the ViziBit seal; administrators can use any seal.
4. **Use.** The integration creates SES requests with the seal id.
5. **Renewal.** Before the certificate expires, the new certificate is installed under the same seal id. Integrations do not change.
6. **Withdrawal.** Removing the seal id from the client stops its use immediately: requests that name it return `403`.

Treat seal ids of organisation seals as confidential.

## Seal without a user interface

```shell
# 1. create the request (as usual)
curl -X POST "https://{{SIGNER_HOST}}/v/1/signature/standalone/integration" \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $ACCESS_TOKEN" -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{ "document": "JVBERi0x…", "signatureLevel": "SES", "sealId": "acme-hr-seal",
        "callbacks": { "onSigned": "https://app.example.com/hooks/s", "onError": "https://app.example.com/hooks/e" } }'

# 2. seal immediately with the returned signature token
curl -X POST "https://{{SIGNER_HOST}}/v/1/signature/standalone/pdf/init" \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $SIGNATURE_TOKEN" -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d '{}'

# 3. download the sealed PDF
curl "https://{{SIGNER_HOST}}/v/1/signature/standalone/integration/$SIGNATURE_REQUEST_ID/document" \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $ACCESS_TOKEN" -o sealed.pdf
```

Your backend can seal documents on its own: create the request and immediately call [Start signing](#start-signing) with the returned signature token and an empty body (optionally with a `signatureImage` for a visible seal). When the call returns `204` the document is sealed; download it right away. The webhook follows as usual.

# Identification of signers without a digital identity

<aside class="warning">
Preview: the request flag and the signing-UI entry point are available; the identification service itself is being integrated. Until it is enabled, starting identification returns <code>501</code> and the SDK tells the signer to use one of the offered signing methods.
</aside>

Signers who do not have a digital identity yet (no certificate with any offered provider) can be identified remotely once and then sign. You decide per request whether this is offered:

* Create the request with `"allowIdentification": true` (QES or AES).
* The SDK then shows **"I don't have a digital identity"** in the provider chooser.
* The SDK calls `POST /pdf/identification`; when identification is available the response contains a `redirectUrl` to which the signer is sent; after identification the signer returns and signs.
* Requests without the flag answer `403`. Requests that already finished answer `409`.

No integration change is needed when identification becomes available.

# Errors

> Error response (RFC 7807 problem details)

```json
{
  "type": "https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc9110#section-15.5.1",
  "title": "Bad Request",
  "status": 400,
  "errorCode": 53,
  "errorMessage": "Provider FinaRiws does not support AES. Supported for AES: LocalComponentV1, SwisscomEtsiV1",
  "additionalInformation": null
}
```

Errors are returned as problem details with three extra fields: `errorCode` (number), `errorMessage` (human-readable) and `additionalInformation`. Branch on `errorCode`. Malformed JSON is rejected with `400` and an `errorMessage` but no `errorCode`.

In webhooks and in `/pdf/state` the same errors appear by **name** (e.g. `"StandaloneIntegrationSignatureRejected"`).

| Code | Name | HTTP | When |
|---|---|---|---|
| 135 | `ErrorContentIsEmpty` | 400 | `document` missing or empty |
| 30014 | `StandaloneIntegrationRequestDocumentIsNotAPdf` | 400 | `document` is not a PDF |
| 30007 | `StandaloneIntegrationRequestOnSignedCallbackMustBeDefined` | 400 | `callbacks.onSigned` missing |
| 30006 | `StandaloneIntegrationRequestOnErrorCallbackMustBeDefined` | 400 | `callbacks.onError` missing |
| 30009 | `StandaloneIntegrationRequestExpiresAtNotValid` | 400 | `expiresAt` in the past or too far ahead |
| 275 | `BadRequest` | 400 | Other validation errors: level, phone number, jurisdiction, callback URL or method, field lengths, invalid usage range |
| 186 | `SignatureProviderNotDefined` | 400 | Providers missing or unknown; provider must be chosen |
| 53 | `UnsupportedSignatureMethod` | 400 | Provider does not support the requested level |
| 205 | `ForbiddenAccessMissingPermissions` | 403 | Seal that is neither `Generic` nor assigned to your client; provider not allowed for the request; signature position locked; identification not allowed |
| 30019 | `StandaloneIntegrationUnauthorized` | 401 / 403 | Missing or wrong token type; managing another client |
| 84 | `EntityNotFound` | 404 | Request does not exist or belongs to another client |
| 30018 | `StandaloneIntegrationInvalidState` | 409 | Action not possible in the current state |
| 183 | `LiteWorkflowActionAlreadyCompleted` | 409 | Document already signed |
| 30016 | `StandaloneIntegrationDocumentDeleted` | 410 | Document already deleted |
| 28 | `CouldNotStartIdentification` | 501 | Identification not available yet |
| 63 | `UnhandledSigningException` | webhook | Provider or signing failure (see `message`) |
| 30003 | `StandaloneIntegrationSignatureRejected` | webhook | Rejected by the signer or by your backend |
| 30004 | `StandaloneIntegrationSignatureWorkflowExpired` | webhook | Expired before it was signed |

# Implementation guide

## Backend: create

1. Obtain and cache an access token.
2. Generate a random webhook secret for the document.
3. Create the signature request with the PDF, your `externalReference`, the signing policy, the webhook URLs, `callbacks.authorizationHeader: "Bearer <secret>"`, an `expiresAt` that fits the use case and, if you want a fixed position, `signatureImage`.
4. Store `signatureRequestId`, `signatureToken`, the secret and `expiresAt` with your document and mark it as *waiting for signature*.

## Frontend: sign

1. When the signer opens the document, your frontend asks **your** backend for the signature token. Return it only to the person who is supposed to sign.
2. Mount the SDK with the token. Pass `signatureText` (or `signatureImage`) if the signer should place the signature, `locale` for the UI language, and `allowReject` if the signer may decline.
3. On `onSuccess`, show a confirmation and refresh your view from your backend. On `onError`, show the message; to retry after `Failed`, `Rejected` or `Expired`, create a new request.

## Backend: complete

1. Receive the webhook, check the `Authorization` header against the stored secret and look up the document by `signatureRequestId` (idempotently).
2. On `success: true`: download the signed PDF, store it and, optionally, validate it (for legally important documents, verify that the signature is qualified and that the signer's certificate belongs to the expected person — the Standalone Signer does not check who signed). Then delete the stored copy.
3. On `success: false`: record `state`, `errorCode` and `message`.
4. Safety net: periodically check requests still waiting close to `expiresAt` with `GET /integration/{id}` in case a webhook was lost.

## Several signatures on one document

One request yields one signature; signatures are added as PDF incremental updates, so earlier signatures stay valid. For *employer signs → employee signs → employer seal*, create the next request with the PDF downloaded from the previous one (with a different signature position). Your application owns the order, reminders, deadlines and signer replacement.

## Choosing expiresAt

`expiresAt` bounds how long the signer has, how long the signature token works and how long the Standalone Signer keeps the PDF. Typical values: a few hours for in-session signing, 7–14 days for documents sent to employees or customers.

## Security checklist

* Client secret only on the backend, in a secret store; rotate it periodically.
* Signature token handed only to the intended signer.
* Webhook authenticated with a per-request `authorizationHeader`; handler idempotent.
* Success acted upon only after the webhook or `GET /integration/{id}`, never on a browser event alone.
* Signed PDF downloaded promptly and the stored copy deleted.
* The signing policy (level, providers, seal) is fixed by your backend; the signing UI cannot weaken it.
* Where it matters legally, validate the signed PDF (signature level and signer identity).

## Go-live checklist

* Production credentials stored; sandbox credentials removed from production configuration.
* Content Security Policy updated for the production Standalone Signer host.
* Webhook endpoint reachable from the Standalone Signer and monitored.
* Monitoring for requests stuck in *waiting for signature* and for webhook failures.
* Test matrix passed in the sandbox:
  * every provider you offer, at every level you use; visible card, your own image and invisible signature;
  * SES with every seal you use (`Generic` and/or your organisation's seal), visible and invisible;
  * signer cancels at the provider → error webhook; **Reject** → `Rejected`; short `expiresAt` → `Expired`;
  * webhook endpoint down → retries → operator re-send;
  * duplicate webhook delivery handled;
  * download after `expiresAt` handled gracefully (`401`/`410`);
  * every UI language you support; provider pages render in iframe mode (otherwise use `mode: 'popup'`).

# Testing

## Sandbox

ViziBit provides a sandbox host and an API client per integration team and tells you which provider test accounts are available there. The ViziBit electronic seal (`Generic`) works in the sandbox without setup; if you need your organisation's seal, ViziBit assigns a test seal to your client.

## SDK test harness

`https://{{SIGNER_HOST}}/signature/standalone/sdk-test.html` runs the complete flow against the host it is served from:

1. **Sign in** with your API client (`client_id` / `client_secret`).
2. **Signature request (your backend):** level, allowed providers, jurisdiction, signer phone number, identification, seal id, external reference, document name and expiry. This is exactly what your backend would send.
3. **SDK options (signing UI):** UI language, preferred provider, provider language, **Reject** button, error propagation.
4. **Signature:** generated card, uploaded image or none, an optional position (PDF points), whether it is sent in the create request (preset by your backend) or as SDK options, and whether its position is locked.
5. **Run** creates a request with a sample PDF and mounts the SDK in an iframe; **Open in window** uses a popup; **Destroy instance** unmounts it.
6. **Use existing token…** mounts the SDK with a signature token created by **your own backend** — the fastest way to test your backend integration before your frontend exists.
7. The **activity log** shows every HTTP call, status event, placement event and callback. After success, **Download signed PDF** fetches the result.

<aside class="notice">
The harness creates requests with callback URLs pointing to the Standalone Signer itself. On hosts where those development receivers are disabled the webhook delivery is reported as failed in the audit log; signing is not affected. Use your own webhook endpoint (or "Use existing token…") to test webhooks.
</aside>

## Dashboard

`https://{{SIGNER_HOST}}/signature/standalone/` lists your signature requests (filter by state or text: id, external reference, document name, provider…). The detail view shows the signing policy, the provider used, timestamps, webhook configuration and the full **audit log** of every state change, webhook attempt and error. The **support identifier** shown in the SDK error dialog is the `signatureRequestId`: search for it here. Administrators can re-send webhooks, force-expire stuck requests and delete stored documents.

# Operations

## Network requirements

| From → to | Purpose |
|---|---|
| Your backend → `https://{{SIGNER_HOST}}` | API calls (token, signature requests, downloads). |
| Signers' browsers → `https://{{SIGNER_HOST}}` | SDK, document preview and signing. |
| Standalone Signer → your webhook endpoints | Webhooks. If you filter incoming traffic, allow the addresses ViziBit gives you for your environment. |
| Signers' browsers → `cdnjs.cloudflare.com` | PDF.js, unless you self-host it (`pdfJsUrl`, `pdfWorkerUrl`). |
| Signers' browsers → provider pages | Consent pages of FINA, Certilia, Swisscom and A-Trust; for card/token signing, the ViziBit local component on the signer's own computer. |

## On-premise installations

The Standalone Signer can also run in your own infrastructure. The API, the SDK and the behaviour described in this guide are the same as in the cloud; only the host differs. ViziBit installs and configures on-premise installations together with your operations team and provides the infrastructure, network and availability requirements during installation planning.

## Data retention

* **Documents** are stored encrypted and deleted when you delete them or when `expiresAt` passes, whichever comes first.
* **Signer phone numbers** are removed from the request as soon as signing starts or the request reaches an outcome; temporary processing records are cleared within 24 hours.
* **Request records** (state, policy, external reference, audit log; no document content) are kept for support and usage reporting.

## Monitoring and support

* `GET https://{{SIGNER_HOST}}/v/1/signature/standalone/health/basic` (liveness) and `/v/1/signature/standalone/health/ready` (readiness).
* Every request has an audit log in the [dashboard](#dashboard) with each state change, webhook attempt and error; the SDK shows signers a support identifier (the `signatureRequestId`) to quote when contacting support.
* ViziBit support can re-send webhooks and expire stuck requests.
* Provider availability (FINA, AKD/Certilia, Swisscom, A-Trust) is outside the Standalone Signer; provider errors are reported in the webhook `message` and the audit log.

# Administration (API clients)

Administrator access tokens can manage API clients; every client can read and rotate its own record. The same functions are available in `https://{{SIGNER_HOST}}/signature/standalone/admin.html`.

```shell
# Create a client with a partner and an assigned seal (admin)
curl -X POST "https://{{SIGNER_HOST}}/v/1/signature/standalone/admin/clients" \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $ADMIN_TOKEN" -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{ "clientId": "acme-hr", "displayName": "ACME HR", "partner": "acme-partner", "sealIds": ["acme-hr-seal"] }'

# Change partner or seals (admin); omitted fields stay unchanged, [] removes all seals
curl -X PATCH "https://{{SIGNER_HOST}}/v/1/signature/standalone/admin/clients/acme-hr" \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $ADMIN_TOKEN" -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{ "sealIds": ["acme-hr-seal", "acme-board-seal"] }'

# Rotate your own secret
curl -X POST "https://{{SIGNER_HOST}}/v/1/signature/standalone/admin/clients/{{CLIENT_ID}}/rotate-secret" \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $ACCESS_TOKEN" -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d '{}'
```

| Endpoint | Access | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| `GET /admin/clients` | admin | List clients. |
| `GET /admin/clients/me` | any client | Your own record. |
| `GET /admin/clients/{clientId}` | own record or admin | One client. |
| `POST /admin/clients` | admin | Create a client: `clientId`, `displayName`, optional `clientSecret` (generated if omitted), `isAdmin`, `partner`, `sealIds`. The secret is returned once. |
| `PATCH /admin/clients/{clientId}` | admin | Change `displayName`, `partner` (`""` clears) and `sealIds` (`[]` clears). |
| `POST /admin/clients/{clientId}/rotate-secret` | own record or admin | New secret (optional `newClientSecret`), returned once. Partner and seals are kept. |
| `DELETE /admin/clients/{clientId}` | admin | Revoke a client. |

Client records contain `clientId`, `displayName`, `isAdmin`, `partner` and `sealIds`. `partner` groups the usage of several clients (for example all customers of one partner); `sealIds` are the organisation seals the client may apply; the ViziBit electronic seal (`Generic`) needs no assignment.
